  -> catch-up: with no data the decoder writes a guessed insight at step j iff eta(j+1)/n > q/(q+1);
     data (t_p > lo) only lower the posterior of t_p <= j, so (C_1) holds for every data set iff
     eta(n-1)/n <= q/(q+1) (equality: depends on ties). For n = 2..6 that is eta <= 0.8 (q=2), 0.9 (q=3), as found.
     Larger n, no data:
       q=2 eta=0.50 n=10: eta(n-1)/n = 0.450 vs q/(q+1) = 0.667; (C_1) fails for 0 of 21 truths
       q=2 eta=0.50 n=50: eta(n-1)/n = 0.490 vs q/(q+1) = 0.667; (C_1) fails for 0 of 101 truths
       q=2 eta=0.66 n=10: eta(n-1)/n = 0.594 vs q/(q+1) = 0.667; (C_1) fails for 0 of 21 truths
       q=2 eta=0.66 n=50: eta(n-1)/n = 0.647 vs q/(q+1) = 0.667; (C_1) fails for 0 of 101 truths
       q=2 eta=0.70 n=10: eta(n-1)/n = 0.630 vs q/(q+1) = 0.667; (C_1) fails for 0 of 21 truths
       q=2 eta=0.70 n=50: eta(n-1)/n = 0.686 vs q/(q+1) = 0.667; (C_1) fails for 51 of 101 truths
       q=2 eta=0.80 n=10: eta(n-1)/n = 0.720 vs q/(q+1) = 0.667; (C_1) fails for 11 of 21 truths
       q=2 eta=0.80 n=50: eta(n-1)/n = 0.784 vs q/(q+1) = 0.667; (C_1) fails for 51 of 101 truths
       q=3 eta=0.50 n=10: eta(n-1)/n = 0.450 vs q/(q+1) = 0.750; (C_1) fails for 0 of 31 truths
       q=3 eta=0.50 n=50: eta(n-1)/n = 0.490 vs q/(q+1) = 0.750; (C_1) fails for 0 of 151 truths
       q=3 eta=0.66 n=10: eta(n-1)/n = 0.594 vs q/(q+1) = 0.750; (C_1) fails for 0 of 31 truths
       q=3 eta=0.66 n=50: eta(n-1)/n = 0.647 vs q/(q+1) = 0.750; (C_1) fails for 0 of 151 truths
       q=3 eta=0.70 n=10: eta(n-1)/n = 0.630 vs q/(q+1) = 0.750; (C_1) fails for 0 of 31 truths
       q=3 eta=0.70 n=50: eta(n-1)/n = 0.686 vs q/(q+1) = 0.750; (C_1) fails for 0 of 151 truths
       q=3 eta=0.80 n=10: eta(n-1)/n = 0.720 vs q/(q+1) = 0.750; (C_1) fails for 0 of 31 truths
       q=3 eta=0.80 n=50: eta(n-1)/n = 0.784 vs q/(q+1) = 0.750; (C_1) fails for 101 of 151 truths
     So (C_1) holds in E3's setting (eta = 1/2) and in Theorem 3's regime (eta = 8 eps <= 1/2), for the
     class-prior majority decoder. It is not a property of the class alone: from a state carrying a wrong
     insight, or an insight on a problem without one, the catch-up human keeps it and the run fails.
  -> silent: (C_1) fails as soon as an insight with t_p < n-1 has not been found.

  Code's decoder_write_step vs exact majority decoder (eta = 1/2, q in {2,3}, n = 2..7, all probe sets):
    silent  : 7680 cases, mismatches = 0
    catch-up: 7680 cases, mismatches = 0
    catch-up mistake set an up-set? k_p unknown: fails in 0/1230; k_p known: fails in 2610/6450
    -> Proposition 4 applies to the decoder that has not yet learned k_p; the binary search must be run
       on that (old) decoder's run, held fixed, as the code does. Once k_p is known the mistake set is
       [t_p, w) or {w}, not an up-set.
