4. A particular human on which end-weighting costs more than twice chained imitation
   States s0..sN (the human's run), z1..zN (an off-track chain), y. Success: final state sN.
   P: s_i -> s_(i+1), z_i -> z_(i+1).   A = P except A(s_(N-1)) = y.   B = P except B(s0) = z1.
   Prior w(P)=3/10, w(A)=2/10, w(B)=4/10, so K(P) = log2(10/3) = 1.737.
   Initially the decoder is right everywhere (P+A outvote B at s0). A query at s_(N-1) refutes A,
   after which B outvotes P at s0 and every run fails until s0 itself is queried.
  N =   4: E[failed runs]  CI =     2.00   EW =     3.59   ratio EW/CI = 1.794   LAST = inf
           Theorem 1 sums E sum q(M_t): CI 0.500, EW 0.664; Theorem 3 sum 0.448; K(P) = 1.737; bound 2nK = 13.9
  N =  16: E[failed runs]  CI =     8.00   EW =    20.14   ratio EW/CI = 2.518   LAST = inf
           Theorem 1 sums E sum q(M_t): CI 0.500, EW 0.816; Theorem 3 sum 0.629; K(P) = 1.737; bound 2nK = 55.6
  N =  64: E[failed runs]  CI =    32.00   EW =    97.56   ratio EW/CI = 3.049   LAST = inf
           Theorem 1 sums E sum q(M_t): CI 0.500, EW 0.923; Theorem 3 sum 0.762; K(P) = 1.737; bound 2nK = 222.3
  N = 256: E[failed runs]  CI =   128.00   EW =   428.49   ratio EW/CI = 3.348   LAST = inf
           Theorem 1 sums E sum q(M_t): CI 0.500, EW 0.974; Theorem 3 sum 0.837; K(P) = 1.737; bound 2nK = 889.3
  -> ratio EW/CI exceeds 2 (tends to 4 as N grows). Theorem 3(a) compares upper bounds (2nK vs nK);
     on a particular human end-weighting can be worse than chained imitation by more than 2.
==============================================================================
5. Theorem 3(c) variants, and the 'doubling loses about log r' remark
  n=64, r=4: (uniform half -> last r) min q = 1.65e-03 = 1/(2nH_n) -> worst case 607*K (vs 2n = 128); last-r weight >= 0.1513 = 1/(2r)
               (harmonic half -> last r) min q = 7.81e-03 = 1/(2n) -> worst case 128*K; last-r weight >= 0.1328 >= 1/(2r)
  n=1024, r=8: (uniform half -> last r) min q = 6.50e-05 = 1/(2nH_n) -> worst case 15379*K (vs 2n = 2048); last-r weight >= 0.0708 = 1/(2r)
               (harmonic half -> last r) min q = 4.88e-04 = 1/(2n) -> worst case 2048*K; last-r weight >= 0.0630 >= 1/(2r)
  Any weighting with q(dist d) >= 1/(C (d+1) log2(d+2)) for all d < n needs C >= S(n) = sum_d 1/((d+1)log2(d+2)):
    n = 2^ 4: S(n) = 1.99
    n = 2^10: S(n) = 2.62
    n = 2^20: S(n) = 3.10
    n = 2^40: S(n) = 3.58
  -> 'a factor of about log r' must carry a further factor growing like log log n (or (log log r)^(1+o(1))).
